Z||000015||Ken Wittlief||05-18-2001||10:19 PM||||Im really curious about this - anyone understand how the car could have 120 psi compression in all four cylinders and be blowing oil out the exhaust? A whole quart in 50 miles?!?<P>  I cant think of a pathway where oil could be coming out the exhaust with out:<P>  A. being burned in the process and coming out as blue smoke<P>  and B. soaking the spark plugs with oil?<P>  I can see a broken ring causing oil to be burned, and maybe a bad valve guide putting oil into a cylinder, and that one plug fouling out till it stops firing, and the oil being blown out the exhaust unburned. If that happened the oil soaked/fouled plug would be pretty obvious.<P>  This is bewildering!||||reg||1
Z||000016||Ken Wittlief||05-18-2001||10:23 PM||||How does the rest of the car look? If worse comes to worse do you think it would be worth putting another engine in the car?||||reg||1
Z||000017||Tigger||05-18-2001||10:56 PM||||Is the Check Engine light coming on?  Is it throwing any codes?||||reg||1
Z||000018||Ken Wittlief||05-18-2001||11:24 PM||||is it possible a bad pcv valve is simply sucking the oil out of the valve cover and blowing it through the engine?||||reg||1
Z||000019||WKDFIRO||05-19-2001||12:46 AM||||It sounds like you might have multiple problems. The backfiring through the Throttle body sure does sound like its badly out of tune. If it was coming out the pipe I would say it sounds like the ignition coil is really having heat problems. If the car starts off good and then deteriorates it does sound like and electronic heat problem. The coil may be worn out and is failing intermittantly due to overheating.  <P>As for the oil questions that we need to know!<P>1) You say its coming out of the tailpipe, smoke? Blue smoke? White smoke? Or the James Bond way?<P>2)How were the plugs when you checked the compression?<P>If you have good compression than the only thing I can think of is valve stem seals with the given info.<P>Yikes what a challenge! <p>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 05-19-2001).]||||reg||4
Z||000020||fieromillie||05-19-2001||01:32 AM||||It is blowing blue smoke out of the tailpipe and lots of it. The plugs looked good when checking the compression. I dont know alot about mechanical things, but enough to know I have been taken. I have a Silver 84 and I just love it. Was adding the Yellow One hopeing to have one to drive leaft in the driveway. Seems whoever is up first at my house takes the little Silver car  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif">) and then I am leaft with the Big Beast (Mercury Marquis) I am afraid he has changed so many things and not knowing alot it is very hard.<BR>It shifts totally different from my other. They are both 84's both 4Cyl, but the one I just got I have to shift so much sooner. Fourth gear at 30 mph, instead of 40 on the other. When I picked it up he was not home. He leaft the keys under the torn up seat he forgot to mention. Had to knock the trash out of the way to get in. It was hard to start, was 2 quarts low on oil before even leaving the drive, the wipers are bad and have one speed. Car ran terrible the fist hour but seemed to get better, at some of the toll stops it would cut off and I would have to get a jump start. I noticed when reaching across the seat that the lighter was extremely hot. It was like there was constant current going to it. The clutch has to be held completely to the floor to start it, and I mean completely. Upon getting home I replaced the battery, seems to be draining this one also. Draining it from just trying to start it. I can get it started but then it backfires out of the carbrator and just dies. Dies before I can even get it moved a foot. I thought I would take a look underneath and tried to push it up on ramps and it came back down, smashing and breaking my toe. When I broke down and my dad came to try to jump it he said that it sounded as thoe it was out of gas. I knew this was impossible because I had just filled it. The gas guage died on the way home from Michigan. His first thought was the fuel pump. He was a Ford mechanic for 30 years but refuses to touch this Fiero.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif">( Says he only knows the old cars, has been retired now for 20 years. I got a pressure guage but it will not fit. Is it possible for the fuel pump to not pump as strong as it should ?? And what kind of pressure guage will fit. This one I am told is for a six cylinder multi port fuel injected. It is hard to believe that this car could be this nightmare  <A HREF="http://cgi.ebay.com/ebaymotors/aw-cgi/ebayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=557463598&r=0&t=0" TARGET=_blank>http://cgi.ebay.com/ebaymotors/aw-cgi/ebayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=557463598&r=0&t=0</A>     ||||reg||1
Z||000021||Mach10||05-19-2001||02:54 AM||||I hate people like that. Here's my advice. Go back to that guy's house at about 3AM and slam a 15-lb axe in his front door, making sur it sticks. Even better if you go through a screen door too After you've done that RUN! Be sure to buy the most generic axe available, and WEAR GLOVES. Trust me, the psychological damage and paranoia will be well worth the 2K. If you keep an eye on him, I guarantee that he'll be requesting Police protection  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Just don't EVER go back to that place.<P>As for the motor, if it's that FUBAR, then drop-replace it. The only thing I can think of is a leaky head gasket, blowing oil only when oil pressure starts building. <P>What a goat-roper. My sympathies. ||||reg||8
Z||000022||maryjane||05-19-2001||04:09 AM||||I would be mad <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"> as **** if this happened to me. It's the peril of buying from the internet. Although I like Mach 10's solution, you might try ebay and the DA's office. Seems like the car was run to death and grossly misrepresented.At the very least it can get him barred from ebay under his real name and maybe give you a little revenge. Everyone on this forum has always given straight answers upfront, don't give up yet. There are people here that will help all they can. Wish I knew half as much as most of them do.Keep us posted. Good luck<BR>Don & Jane ||||reg||1
Z||000023||thomas_l||05-19-2001||07:14 AM||||Looks like you may as well deal with it because your only recourse is small claims court in another state. Maybe the Michigan crowd can offer local advice. It seems to me that if you carefully piece together a timeline, it would be clear to a judge that this guy went out of his way to make sure he got your money and you got the car sight unseen.<P>Next you need to get it fixed. If you don't even have a floor jack available you're gonna have to get someone to help or get ready to pay the man to fix it. Make note of all expenses and such in case you sue. Anyway, the engine thing isn't sounding good. Yes rotten valve guides/seals can suck odwn some oil but more likely worn or coked up piston rings for that much oil. But that isn't what's causing it not to run. You can check fuel pressure on that car with a plain old vac/pressure gauge. The range is 9-13 psi, most I have seen run 11-12. To make it go you need fuel, fire, and timing. You said the plugs are dry and the module checked good. You see fuel spray coming out of the injector. The only thing left is timing. If it ran for while, the ignition timing isn't that far off. If it has gotten worse and worse and the distributor has not been moved, the cam gear might have slipped a tooth or two but not completely gone. It could even have a marginal pick up coil that isn't triggering the module right. You are gonna need to get a shop manual and sweet talk dad into getting back on the case or plan on paying someone to at least diagnose the problem.<P>The battery drain is a tough one. You need to put an ammeter inline with a battery cable (this will tell you how bad the draw is) then pull fuses one at a time until the amps read near zero. Don't forget to stick a wood block or something in the door switch so your dome light doesn't give you a false reading. Look for simple stuff too - shorted cig lighter, map lights on, trunk light, etc.<P>Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000024||mrfixit58||05-19-2001||08:35 AM||||If the motor is using that much oil my first reaction is a broken ring or cracked head. But, the 120 compression seems to refute that. First, look at something simple. Is the PCV valve plugged? Excessive crankcase pressure can force oil past the rings. Usually when it get as bad as you've described, the pressure will blow the dip-stick out of the tube. You didn't mention if this was happening.<P>Next, check the timing. Dukes are notorious for eating the fiber timing gear. The parts to fix this is cheap, the labor isn't.<P>Try another compression check. This time, listen for air leaking out of the cylinders. If the sound comes out of the oil filler cap, it's a sign the rings or piston in that cylinder are bad. If you hear it out of the exhaust, it's usually the exhaust valve or seat or a cracked head. If you hear it out of the carburetor (or throttle body), it's the intake valve, seat, or cracked head.<P>The other thing it could be is missing valve stem seals. You mentioned that the motor doesn't leak ANY oil. That could be a hint that the motor has recently been worked over. Is it possible that the head could have been replaced, valve work done, and the stem seals forgotten?<P>I'm not experience with the Dukes but the above are some generic engine diagnostic procedures that should apply.<P><P>------------------<BR>Roy :D<BR>87 GT  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG"> ||||reg||4
Z||000025||SteveJ||05-19-2001||12:30 PM||||You drove this car 400 miles and it wasn't backfiring out the throttle but now it does and won't start. You have had the plugs out and looked at 'em. So forgive a dumb question but do you have the wires back on the right plugs? That would cause the backfire and nonstart.||||reg||1
Z||000026||Songman||05-19-2001||12:46 PM||||She said it was backfiring within 50 miles of her leaving the guys house....<P>I agree with mrfixit on the plan of action to get this thing rolling. Hopefully, that part of it is something simple and you can turn this nightmare into something that is not so bad...<P>Sorry that you have had this kind of experience. You have come to the right place now to help make sure it doesn't happen again. There are always good Fieros for sale here at good prices. And from people who will do you right... I would have sold you my 88 coupe that only needs a cam gear to be perfect for less than you gave for that 84.<P>Welcome to the forum and keep us up on what happens with the car. Between all of us, we can get you on the road. Maybe we can even figure out a way to get all of those stickers off without messing up the paint. It wasn't a bad looking little car before he added those...<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000027||Songman||05-19-2001||12:49 PM||||One more thing... How about all 2400+ members of this board inundate this losers e-mail with messages about how awful he did this nice lady....<P>We gotta protect each other...<P>Send to:<BR>Hartmafam@aol.com <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000028||theogre||05-19-2001||12:56 PM||||First,,, Go around to your local parts stores and find a haynes or chiltons book. These have tables you follow a step at a time... those should help.<P>I don't think you need a new motor. The backfire like that is very likely something silly most of the time. 2 plug wires swapped, bad distributor cap, timing slipped, that kind of thing.<P>Also check all the vacuum lines and everything connected to them. A vac leak can cause all sorts of problems, depending just where it is. You may have knocked a line off something.<P>120psi compression, but it burns oil like gas.... I'd be looking at the valves and head. If the oil drains are plugged too much oil could be staying in the valve area. That could make it burn oil as described. At idle the drains may pass enough oil to let it stop.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 05-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000029||Mach10||05-19-2001||01:45 PM||||Songman: YOU EVIL!!! &gt;:}<P>I second that. Everyone start messaging that clown. He's got AOHell, so it'll be "yougotmail-you-you-you-yougot-you-yougotmai-yougotmail" for the next 3 hours. I LIKE!<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR><p>[This message has been edited by Mach10 (edited 05-19-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000030||lowCG||05-19-2001||04:24 PM||||Getting burned really sucks,but I would check into what Ebay has to say about recourse since there appear to be many issues here.You're not going to feel very good about having to fix everything,and paying the price for the car as well.<BR>How did you make your payment to this ah...individual?<BR>I just bought my Lexus SC400 from a guy in Texas;wound up flying down there from Seattle just because I figured I would only be risking the trip that way,but I was still nervous.I found that the online escrow services used when having a car delivered by truck wouldn't even handle the title(!)so I could've really been buying a stolen vehicle.||||reg||1
Z||000031||fieromillie||05-19-2001||05:03 PM||||I bought the car by making 4 payments one every two weeks. I guess I kept looking at the pic and thinking that no one would keep the outside that nice and not keep everything else going. I sure have learned a lesson here. Can't trust anything.When I saw what the car was like I could of just stood there and cried. I worked very hard to get that money together. It was an extra job that I did to pull together the money. Has anyone ever heard of useing a Fuel Injector Cleaning Kit. Not sure how it works but somehow this will make it run long enough to get it to a shop so that it wont have to be towed. Has anyone heard of this ?? Maybe I should start thinking about at least getting it looked out to find out the problem. Then see if it is anything that I can possibly fix. If anyone has heard of using the Fuel Injector cleaning kit please let me know, and thanks so much for everyone's help.I am pleased to be part of this Forum, Ann   ||||reg||1
Z||000032||deceler8||05-19-2001||05:11 PM||||Funny how this guy makes no statements about how the car runs but does state that he did replace the head gasket...if he did it himself it's entirely possible he screwed it up.<P>I'd contact the Michigan Department of Consumer Affairs and see if they have any suggestions. Most states take this kind of Internet fraud very seriously and it's possible they can assist you. ||||reg||1
Z||000033||starfighter007@msn.com||05-19-2001||06:41 PM||||I am really sorry to hear about your situation I can understand start to get over this how over whelmed you must feel but you do need to get over this bad situation and start finding all the good things about the carfirst thing is sounds like the trans is a-ok I ll bet you dad could help diognose this oil lose prblem your having and the back firing problem.I m sure between all the good people here you and your dad  youll have your car running in tip top condition for next to nothing these problems you discribe I bet will turn out to be minor things I m curious about how handy you are with wrenches?In any case I hope your dad will give you a hand if we all pull together things will turn out fine.good luck and cheer-up ||||reg||10
Z||000034||fieromillie||05-20-2001||12:37 AM||||No, I guess I am not good with wrenches. My father was a Ford Mechanic for 32 years and never owned anything but. But he is tired and just doesent even want to talk about this Fiero. Even thoe he is still saying, it is something to do with not getting enough fuel. Am not really close to my brother anymore but he is very mechanically inclined. Owns a 37 Hudson. Does all his own work right down to chopping and upholstry.<BR>Was even on ESPN last year. But no I guess I am not that good with a wrench, am trying to learn thoe. Just wish it wouldnt bug me so bad to see that Fiero sitting in the driveway. Thanks for reminding me to look for the positive. Maybe it would be easier if I quit looking out in the driveway.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif">)||||reg||1
Z||000035||Ken Wittlief||05-20-2001||12:54 AM||||I agree with the last post. Its possible the previous owner was a lousy mechanic and messed up a few things, and with a little help you might have a great car on your hands within a short amount of time.<P>  the best way you can get even with this guy is to do what he could not, turn the car back into a great little machine.<P>  I think the best lesson out of this for anyone else is to never buy a car until you see it in person and drive it for 10 or 20 miles at least, and poke your nose around the whole thing.<P>  Believe me, you are not the first person to get the short end of the stick when buying a used car. My first car, a VW, had a loud muffler, and when it idled the oil light flickered. The salesman told me thats cause the idle was too low. <P>  Turns out it was really because a main bearing had spun when the previous owner ran it out of oil. I ended up rebuilding the engine about a year later when it threw a connecting rod.<P> I bought a 69 cougar when I was stationed in florida in '77 (USCG). The owner wouldnt let me drive it on the highway, said his insurace had expired, but he let me drive it on city streets. When I was looking the car over he pointed out the tires were 6 months old and said, look at these front tires, there cant be anything wrong with the front end of this car or the tires would have worn out already.<P>   It struck me as odd the way he said it, kinda defensively. When I got it licensed it was kinda squirrly at 70mph, you had to steer it constantly - which I attributed to it having power steering. A year later the front tires were bald, I got new ones and had the front end aligned. The mechanic called me in and showed me he had the castor (camber?) all the way to the end and the tops of the wheels were still leaning in towards the motor about 3° out of spec.<P>  Turns out '69 cougars and mustangs have a problem, the frame is not strong enough, and after a few years you need to build a subframe over the engine to keep the shock towers from bending inward. I could have had the subframe put in, but it wasnt worth it. I figure I lost about $1000 on that car - sold it to a mechanic in a garage (I told him what was wrong with it, he was gonna fix it up and sell it - he had the resources - I didnt).<P>  I looked at a Fiero about a year ago that a car collector had in storage. It looked nice and had low mileage. He wouldnt let me drive it, said his insurance wouldnt cover me?!!? He took me for a short ride in it instead. I made up the old "I have other cars to look at too" and never called him back.<P>  the fiero I did buy, just a few weeks ago, wasnt running when I went to look at it, it had been sitting for two years. The owner was in poor health and was having a hard time working on it. It was about a hours drive away for me, and I was thinking of getting a dolly and buying it as it was, even thought it would not start and I could not test drive it at all.<P>  I thought about it, and told the owner I would buy it for $1000 instead of $1500 if we couldnt get it started in his driveway - and I would tow it home. I knew it was a big risk, but the rest of the car is nice (except for the paint).<P>  But he stuck to his guns, he knew what he had, and we did get it to run, took my test drive, and now I own it. So I almost broke the prime directive myself and bought a car without driving it first - I know how temping it is, and you want to believe everything the owner tells you.<P>  I wish you lived closer, I would be willing to help you put your car in proper running condition. There must be some other fiero owners in your area that can get you pointed in the right direction and make your restoration as smooth as possible.<P>  You managed to find this forum, so you are off to a good start. BTW if you really want to work on the car yourself go to the library main branch and see if you can get the Pontaic factory service manual. I got one today from my library (actually let me check it out for 3 weeks too!)<P>  the factory manuals are AWESOME!!!<P>  Just to let you know, the seats on Fieros do tend to wear out and tear, I think partly cause the car is so low we tend to slide into the seat.<P>  I really feel for you, the worst part must have been when you got there and saw the car - the coward didnt even have the guts to face you and left the keys and ran and hid somewhere.<P>  How many members are on this forum? we should all send a few bucks to milly, or donate spare parts and stuff to get her car running like a fine swiss watch. Then she can send the guy a letter and a photo of the car, and tell him she found out the car had the pvc valve in backwards, and now it runs 0 to 60 in 6 seconds, and that two people have already offered her $7000 for the car, but she wont part with it (c:<P>   dont worry too much about getting even with the guy - people like that bring on their own punishment - in fact he was probabally abused as a child.||||reg||1
Z||000036||Songman||05-20-2001||02:29 AM||||OK, who here is good with Dukes and lives in PA? I'm feeling pretty bad about all of this. The little lady needs some help here.<P>Wish I didn't live so far away, Ann, or I'd offer to come take a look at it...<P>Anybody still got CarFax? We oughta run the numbers on this car and see if it really comes up clean.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif">  <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A><p>[This message has been edited by Songman (edited 05-20-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000037||Topknot||05-20-2001||06:18 AM||||Hallo fieromillie,<P>Well I've read each and every reply in your posting here.  First off, I do feel your pain and anguish over wanting to have a nice little car to enjoy, being I just got an 86 GT and still trying to figure out what's up with the clutch.<P>But on the other hand.  I have an 86 SE 4cyl that I am stripping down to the bare bones and then calling in a scrapper to tag the rest away for crunching. Sooooo....if you need any 4 cyl part from an Iron Duke engine just let me know.  Other than helping to pay the shipping..(I live in florida) you can have any part or parts you might need.  The head was a new one I bought about 2 years ago before the car was tossed into the garage about 1 1/2 years ago.<P>If you are truly interested or serious about getting your "dream" car up and running and short on $$$'s then give me an email at hawkster@netwide.net within the next two days being that I am stripping down the car this Sunday!!<P>I live in Orlando Florida, so there will be a little bit of UPS shipping involved to get you any parts you need, but if they can be of help, it's better to give them to you verse having it all crushed at the scrap yard!!<P>Topknot =]<BR>||||reg||1
