Z||000043||Taijiguy||05-20-2001||10:35 AM||||I just skimmed the numerous posts here, and I noticed a few obvious things that were missing:<P>Have you done a standard tune-up? Air filter, plugs, wires, cap and rotor? Any of those things will absolutely cause the symptoms you described.<P>What's it look like inside the air filter housing? Is there any oil standing within the housing? <BR>I just had a lightbulb moment, a problem that could potentially cause all of your problems:<BR>Try driving it without the air filter housing.<BR>Disconnect and remove the whole thing, and try it. It ocurred to me, the cold air intake could be plugged. If that's the case, It would completely screw up intake air flow, and make it run rich, and could potentially suck oil right out of the breather tube.<BR>Additionally, you can easily check to see if there's excessive blow-by (combustion gases blowing past the rings) by removing the oil filler cap with the engine running. Place your hand over the opening, and then remove it. If the idle changes, you have blow-by sufficient to cause these problems. I doubt that's the case, since blow-by almost 100% of the time will cause leaks more than oil comsumption. But I would try the air filter housing thing first. If it runs OK with it off, you just need to determine if the hose from the side vent is blocked, kinked, or otherwise obstructing air flow to the air filter.<P>I sure hope this is it. It would sure be a simple fix...<BR>~Taiji<P><p>[This message has been edited by Taijiguy (edited 05-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000044||Topknot||05-20-2001||11:22 AM||||Hallo fieromillie,<P>No Problem, Today is Sunday and I have off tomorrow so I have already started the strip down process on the old beauty.<P>So anything that pertains to the engine and such I'll store in the garage until furthur responce from ya.  Just let me know what you might need and there your's.<P>Topknot =]<P>BTW...Also going for doing a full clutch disc etc replacement today.  Don't have all the "major" tools like engine lifts etc but I think with a little Backyard stuff I might be able to accomplish this seeing how I was able to replace the shreaded timing gear on the other hooptie with nothing more than some standard hand tools and a few pieces of 2x4's!! LOL<P><BR>||||reg||1
