Z||000051||mrfixit58||05-21-2001||04:46 PM||||Have you check the catalytic converter to see is it's plugged? I agree with (Ogre?). Double check the ALL vacuum lines. <BR><P>------------------<BR>Roy :D<BR>87 GT  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG"> ||||reg||4
Z||000052||Ken Wittlief||05-21-2001||05:04 PM||||some of those vacuum lines are REALLY hard to check - see my creature damage thread<P>I recommend you go out and get a dental mirror and a small flash lite and trace every vacuum line until you can see both ends and the whole length of it. dont forget the ones on the vapor canister and the cruse control soup can (if you have cruise).<P> something else that works - get a couple of feet of new flexible vacuum hose, and pull the vacuum lines off the intake manifold (plenium) one at a time and put the new hose on it and suck on it till it sticks to your tongue. It should stay stuck for several seconds. If it wont stick to your tongue then that line is leaking somewhere.<P>  there are a couple lines this doesnt work on, ones that go to the air filter and I think one on the vapor canister - but the ones that are important - the ones that will cause problems if they are leaking should be able to hold a vacuum for several seconds. <P>  If I had done this on the vacuum line to the MAP sensor 2 weeks ago I would have found the hole the mouse chewed in it 2 weeks ago, instead messing around with a whole other bunch of stuff, and replacing the MAP sensor and the o2 sensor, chasing ground straps, checking the timing, trying to pull my injectors off to test for leaks. <P>  It is entirely possible that a vacuum line popped off about 50 miles into your maiden voyage homeward and that is when the car started to backfire and act up on you.||||reg||1
Z||000053||starfighter007@msn.com||05-21-2001||05:40 PM||||I am still a little leary when ever you haveback firing thru the carb I always felt that it is a timing problem of some sort.either distrbuter timing is off sending aspark into the cly.before piston is at top dead center and both valves are closed.and or valve timing is off due to bad timing chain that has slipped.but you said that a nice gentleman came and checked your timing with a timing light. I will not second guess him.now somebody posted that it could be a cat. convertervery possible.what is a easy way of checking the cat to see if it is clogged other than cutting open the exhaust pipe and looking,or is that the only way.and while I was writing this post it occured to me that I had a car a couple years ago that did something very similar to whats happening or happened to her car&gt;I pulled out of the drive way dont remember how far I drove whe the car started running ruff stumbeleing and backfiring thru the intakeany how it turned out to be the hose came disconnected from the mass air flow meter or sensor any way my neiober spotted it this large hos disconnected and he pushed it back on  I had my doubts about that hose because I"m thinking timing chain and he dont Know jack about any thing so he insited I start It so to get this over with  my idiot neiobor and make him happy I try to start it.well you guessed it fired right up.  so I"m not sure if your car has one of these mass air flow meters or not as I have a v-6.but maybe some one else could elaborate a little more on this .also you half to half to get a haynes manual!!! this will be the best $20.00 dollors you spend for your little baby they have lots of pictures in it explain procedures if you feel like doing some things you self.any way lets hope this will be a minor thing||||reg||3
